// The other direction: a callback handed BACK. The host calls this symbol and keeps what it returns, so
// a kama function returned crosses exactly as an argument does — and no call site exists for
// `checkForeignCrossing` to watch, which is why the `expose` boundary has to ask. The RETURN only: a
// callback arriving as a PARAMETER is C's own function pointer, and C owes kama no contract for it.
fnptr int32 Op(int32 x);

fn int32 cb(int32 x) { return x + 1; }

expose fn Op kama_get_cb() { return cb; }   // ERROR: `Op` says nothing about its thread

fn int32 main() { return 0; }
